course: dictionary
lesson: 98
title: Addictive: wanting more and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe something that makes you want it again and again. Addictive is one of them. Something addictive is so strong that people keep wanting it and find it hard to stop. «Nicotine is very addictive, so smokers feel uncomfortable until they have the next one.» The clue is the feeling: they want the next one, and stopping is difficult.
explain: Addictive has a lighter meaning too. People say a game or a TV show is addictive when it is so enjoyable that you keep wanting more. «This game is so addictive» is usually a joke about how hard it is to put down. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Notice the phrases it often joins: «highly addictive», «an addictive substance», «an addictive game». A substance or drug is the serious use, while a game is usually the informal one. Ask yourself: is this a health warning, or a bit of fun? The context tells you.
explain: «Vaping ... is less harmful than smoking but not harmless, and still addictive.» Less harmful does not mean safe. The word «still» tells you that something can be addictive even when it is not the worst choice. Always use the whole sentence, not just one word.
fact: Addictive is an adjective: it describes a thing, as in «an addictive game».
fact: «Addictive» means something that makes people want it again and again until they cannot easily stop.
fact: Informally, «addictive» can mean very enjoyable, so that you keep wanting more.
fact: Nicotine, found in cigarettes and vapes, is addictive, and it can harm the body and make it hard to stop.
fact: The context tells you which meaning is used: a health lesson means the serious one, and a joke about a game means the informal one.
exercise: Nicotine is «very addictive». What does addictive mean here? | *Makes people want it again and again until they cannot easily stop | Cheap and easy to buy | Very funny, so people laugh a lot | Hard to see because it is so small
exercise: «This game is so addictive.» What does the speaker mean? | It is a game only for grown-ups | It is dangerous to your body | *It is so enjoyable that you keep wanting more | It is very quiet
exercise: Vaping is «less harmful than smoking but not harmless, and still addictive». What does this tell you? | Vaping is completely safe | Vaping is worse than smoking | *Less harmful is not the same as safe, and vaping can still be addictive | Vaping is a kind of game
test: Nadia says addictive just means a game you like a lot. Why might a health lesson use the word in a different way? | Because health lessons never talk about games | *Because some substances, like nicotine, make people want them again and again and find it hard to stop | Because the word means cheap
test: Why does the author add «still» in «less harmful than smoking but not harmless, and still addictive»? | To show that vaping is a kind of game | To show that vaping is more harmful than smoking | *To show that vaping can be addictive even though it is less harmful than smoking | Vaping has no effect on the body at all
exam: Which sentence uses addictive in its informal meaning? | «Vapes contain nicotine, which is addictive.» | «Smokers can find it very hard to stop when a drug is addictive.» | «Nicotine is an addictive substance that harms the body.» | *«That new puzzle app is so addictive I played it all night.»
